From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fltmgr, ndis, usbd, hidparse: the kernel-driver stubs

fltmgr.sys: the push lock routines were logging stubs, so a minifilter's
locking did nothing. FltInitializePushLock, FltAcquirePushLockExclusive, the
new FltAcquirePushLockShared and FltReleasePushLock are a real lock in the
caller's pointer-sized storage (bit 0 held exclusively, the rest counting
shared holders, waiters sleep on the address); one release routine ends either
kind of hold; not recursive; all as documented. FltRegisterFilter returned the
constant 0xdeadbeaf for every filter though the page says the pointer
identifies the minifilter uniquely: it allocates a filter object now;
FltStartFiltering fails a second call with STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER
(documented); FltUnregisterFilter frees it. There is still no volume to attach
to and no I/O to pass to the callbacks.

ndis.sys: NdisAllocateMemoryWithTag failed always; it allocates non-paged pool
(NDIS_STATUS_FAILURE when that fails), with NdisFreeMemory. NdisAllocateSpinLock
did nothing; the NDIS spin lock routines (Allocate, Free, Acquire, Release)
work on the KSPIN_LOCK and the saved IRQL in NDIS_SPIN_LOCK. ndis.sys gets an
import library so drivers (and the tests) can link to it.

usbd.sys: USBD_ValidateConfigurationDescriptor checked a made-up lower bound.
It now validates by level as documented: 1 the header (type, length, total
length against the buffer), 2 every descriptor in the set (lengths, endpoint
addresses, interface numbers, number of interfaces), 3 additionally the
number of endpoints per interface, the specified descriptor sizes and
sequential interface numbers; other levels are USBD_STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER.
*Offset points at the descriptor that failed.

hidparse.sys (and hid.dll, built from the same source): the report
descriptor's Delimiter local item aborted parsing. The usages inside a
delimiter set are alternatives for one control; the first (a usage, or a
minimum with its maximum) is used, the rest ignored, as the HID class
definition says applications that cannot choose should do.
---
